// AAMC-aligned flashcards — Chem/Phys biochemistry: original cards written to fill the C/P section's biochemistry
// (about 25% of AAMC C/P). Each card's "from" names the Practice Exam card (data/qb-aamc-fl.js) that tests the same
// concept. No AAMC passages, questions or explanations are reproduced.
// Format: {s: tag, disc, aamc: content category, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, src, from}
const QB_AAMC_BIOCHEM = [
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Free histidine has pKa values of 1.8 (α-COOH), 6.0 (side-chain imidazole) and 9.2 (α-NH₃⁺). What is its predominant net charge in a buffer at pH 4.0, and in a buffer at pH 7.4?',choices:['+1 at pH 4.0; +1 at pH 7.4','+2 at pH 4.0; +1 at pH 7.4','0 at pH 4.0; 0 at pH 7.4','+1 at pH 4.0; 0 at pH 7.4'],ans:3,exp:'A group is protonated when the **pH is below its pKa**.\n\n**pH 4.0:** above 1.8 → COO⁻ (−1); below 6.0 → imidazolium (+1); below 9.2 → NH₃⁺ (+1). Net **+1**.\n**pH 7.4:** COO⁻ (−1); above 6.0 → neutral imidazole (0); NH₃⁺ (+1). Net **0**.\n\n+2 then +1: leaves the α-carboxyl protonated, although both pH values are well above 1.8.\n0 at both: treats the imidazole as neutral even at pH 4.0, two units below its pKa.\n+1 at both: keeps the imidazole protonated at pH 7.4, which is above its pKa.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A1',from:'pe3-cp:33'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Free aspartate is dissolved in a buffer at pH 6.0. Its pKa values are 2.0 (α-COOH), 3.9 (side-chain COOH) and 9.8 (α-NH₃⁺). What is the predominant net charge of the molecule?',choices:['+1','−1','0','−2'],ans:1,exp:'Compare pH 6.0 with each pKa. **α-COOH (2.0): pH above → COO⁻, −1. Side-chain COOH (3.9): pH above → COO⁻, −1. α-NH₃⁺ (9.8): pH below → NH₃⁺, +1.** Net = **−1**.\n\n0: counts only the zwitterion and leaves the side-chain carboxyl protonated, which happens only below pH 3.9.\n−2: deprotonates the α-amino group, which needs pH above 9.8.\n+1: protonates both carboxyl groups, which happens only below pH 2.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A2',from:'pe6-cp:42'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Glutamate has pKa values of 2.2 (α-COOH), 4.3 (side-chain COOH) and 9.7 (α-NH₃⁺). In isoelectric focusing, at approximately what pH would free glutamate stop migrating?',choices:['7.0','5.4','3.3','6.0'],ans:2,exp:'A molecule stops migrating at its **pI, where net charge = 0**. For glutamate the neutral form (COO⁻, side-chain COOH, NH₃⁺) exists between the two carboxyl pKa values, so **pI = (2.2 + 4.3) ÷ 2 ≈ 3.3**.\n\n6.0: averages the α-COOH and α-NH₃⁺ pKa values, the rule for an amino acid with no ionizable side chain.\n7.0: averages the two pKa values that flank the −1 form, not the neutral form.\n5.4: averages all three pKa values.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A3',from:'pe6-cp:42'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Lysine has pKa values of 2.2 (α-COOH), 9.0 (α-NH₃⁺) and 10.5 (side-chain NH₃⁺). At which pH does free lysine carry no net charge?',choices:['5.6','6.4','7.2','9.8'],ans:3,exp:'The neutral form (COO⁻, one NH₃⁺, one NH₂) lies between the two **amino** pKa values, so **pI = (9.0 + 10.5) ÷ 2 ≈ 9.8**. Below 9.0 both amines are protonated (+1 net); above 10.5 both are neutral (−1 net).\n\n5.6: averages the α-COOH and α-NH₃⁺ values, ignoring the basic side chain.\n6.4: averages the α-COOH and side-chain values.\n7.2: averages all three pKa values.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A4',from:'pe6-cp:29'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A mixture of two proteins, X (pI 5.0) and Y (pI 8.5), is loaded in the middle of a native gel buffered at pH 7.0 and an electric field is applied. Which outcome is expected?',choices:['X moves toward the cathode and Y moves toward the anode','X moves toward the anode and Y moves toward the cathode','Both move toward the anode, with X moving farther','Neither moves, since pH 7.0 lies between the two pI values'],ans:1,exp:'**pH above pI → net negative; pH below pI → net positive.** At pH 7.0, X (pI 5.0) is negative and moves to the **anode (+)**; Y (pI 8.5) is positive and moves to the **cathode (−)**.\n\nThe reverse pairing swaps the sign rule.\nBoth toward the anode treats Y as negative, but pH 7.0 is below its pI.\nNo movement would require the buffer pH to equal each protein\'s pI, not lie between them.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A5',from:'pe6-cp:29'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5C',q:'The net charge of a protein was calculated at several pH values: pH 3.0, +9; pH 5.0, +3; pH 6.5, 0; pH 8.0, −4; pH 10.0, −10. What is the protein\'s pI, and toward which electrode does it move in a gel run at pH 8.0?',choices:['pI 3.0; toward the anode','pI 6.5; toward the anode','pI 6.5; toward the cathode','pI 8.0; it does not move'],ans:1,exp:'The pI is where net charge crosses **zero: pH 6.5**. At pH 8.0 the protein is **−4**, so it moves to the **positive electrode, the anode**.\n\npI 6.5 with movement to the cathode: right pI, but a negative protein is drawn to the anode, not the cathode.\npI 8.0, no movement: reads the gel pH as the pI; the charge at 8.0 is −4, not 0.\npI 3.0: picks the most positive point, not the zero crossing.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A6',from:'pe3-cp:45'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'An engineered variant of a cytosolic enzyme differs from the original at three sites: Glu12 is now Lys, Asn58 is now Asp, and His77 is now Gln. Using side-chain pKa values Glu 4.1, Lys 10.5, Asp 3.9 and His 6.0 (Asn, Gln: none), how does the variant\'s net charge at pH 7.4 compare with the original\'s?',choices:['It increases by 3','It increases by 2','It increases by 1','It does not change'],ans:2,exp:'At pH 7.4: Glu −1, Lys +1, Asp −1, His ≈ 0 (pH is 1.4 units above its pKa, so only ≈4% protonated), Asn and Gln 0.\n**Glu→Lys: −1 → +1 = +2. Asn→Asp: 0 → −1 = −1. His→Gln: 0 → 0 = 0.** Total **+1**.\n\nNo change: counts His as +1, so its loss looks like −1.\nIncreases by 3: gives the Asn→Asp change the wrong sign.\nIncreases by 2: overlooks the new negative charge from Asp.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A7',from:'pe1-cp:35'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A drug with a carboxylate group (−1 at pH 8.0) binds an enzyme through a salt bridge. The pocket is lined by His (side-chain pKa 6.0), Arg (12.5), Asp (3.9) and Tyr (10.1). At pH 8.0, replacing which residue with alanine would most weaken this salt bridge?',choices:['Histidine','Aspartate','Tyrosine','Arginine'],ans:3,exp:'A salt bridge with a negative carboxylate needs a **fully positive** partner. At pH 8.0 a group is mostly protonated only if its **pKa is above 8.0**. Arginine (12.5) is essentially 100% **+1**, so removing it breaks the salt bridge.\n\nHistidine: pH 8.0 is two units above 6.0, so it is only ≈1% protonated and mostly neutral.\nAspartate: negative at pH 8.0; it would repel the drug, not pair with it.\nTyrosine: its phenol is neutral at pH 8.0 and could at most H-bond.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A8',from:'pe1-cp:23'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A 20-residue peptide whose only ionizable side chains are six histidines (pKa ≈ 6.0) is loaded onto a cation-exchange resin carrying –SO₃⁻ groups (pKa < 1) at several pH values. Percent of peptide retained: pH 4.5, 98%; pH 5.5, 85%; pH 6.5, 25%; pH 7.5, 3%. What best explains the loss of binding as the pH rises?',choices:['The sulfonate groups gain H⁺, so the resin loses its charge','The imidazole rings lose H⁺ and take on a negative charge','The imidazole rings lose H⁺, removing the positive charge','The C-terminal carboxyl loses H⁺ and is repelled by the resin'],ans:2,exp:'Retention falls by half between pH 5.5 and 6.5, centered near **pH 6.0, the histidine pKa**. Below it each imidazole is **protonated (+1)**, so the peptide carries several positive charges that are **attracted** to the –SO₃⁻ resin. Above it the imidazoles are **neutral**, the peptide is nearly uncharged, and nothing holds it on the column.\n\nNegative charge: losing H⁺ from an imidazolium gives a neutral ring; the ring N–H does not ionize until far above pH 7.5.\nSulfonate protonation: with a pKa below 1, the resin stays –SO₃⁻ across the whole range, and gaining H⁺ would need a lower pH, not a higher one.\nC-terminal carboxyl: its pKa is about 2, so it is already COO⁻ at every pH tested and cannot cause a change near pH 6.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A9',from:'pe1-cp:25'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'An enzyme\'s catalytic residue must be protonated to act as a general acid. Relative activity: pH 3.0, 93%; pH 4.0, 56%; pH 5.0, 11%; pH 6.0, 1%. Which residue is it most likely to be?',choices:['Lysine, side-chain pKa near 10.5','Glutamate, side-chain pKa near 4.1','Histidine, side-chain pKa near 6.0','Cysteine, side-chain pKa near 8.3'],ans:1,exp:'Activity follows the **fraction protonated**, so the pH of half-maximal activity equals the residue\'s **pKa**. Half activity falls just above pH 4.0, which matches **glutamate (≈4.1)**.\n\nHistidine: activity would be ≈50% at pH 6.0, but it is 1%.\nCysteine: would stay mostly protonated, and active, up to about pH 8.\nLysine: would stay protonated across this whole pH range.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A10',from:'pe5-cp:32'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A cysteine thiol in an enzyme has a side-chain pKa of 8.3. At pH 7.3, approximately what percentage of this cysteine is in the deprotonated thiolate (–S⁻) form?',choices:['91%','1%','9%','50%'],ans:2,exp:'**Henderson–Hasselbalch:** [S⁻]/[SH] = 10^(pH − pKa) = 10^(7.3 − 8.3) = 10⁻¹ = 1/10.\nFraction S⁻ = 1/(1 + 10) ≈ **9%**.\n\n50%: true only when pH = pKa.\n91%: the protonated fraction, the inverse of what was asked.\n1%: uses a two-unit gap; a one-unit gap gives a 10:1 ratio.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A11',from:'pe5-cp:31'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A ligand-binding pocket is lined only by the side chains of leucine, isoleucine, valine and phenylalanine. Which group on a ligand would interact most favorably with this pocket?',choices:['A carboxylate group','A hydroxyl group','A protonated amine','A cyclohexyl ring'],ans:3,exp:'All four side chains are **nonpolar hydrocarbons**. They pack best against another nonpolar surface through **hydrophobic contacts and dispersion forces**. The cyclohexyl ring is all C and H.\n\nCarboxylate: charged; it would lose its water shell with no ionic partner to replace it.\nHydroxyl: polar; it finds no H-bond partner among these side chains.\nProtonated amine: charged, with the same desolvation cost as the carboxylate.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A12',from:'pe4-cp:36'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Which single substitution on the surface of a soluble protein would most likely decrease its solubility in water at pH 7.4?',choices:['Asp → Glu','Ser → Thr','Val → Glu','Lys → Leu'],ans:3,exp:'Surface residues stay soluble by interacting with water; **charged** side chains do so most strongly. **Lys (+1) → Leu (nonpolar)** removes a charge and adds a hydrophobic patch on the surface.\n\nSer → Thr: both have an uncharged –OH, so polarity barely changes.\nVal → Glu: replaces a nonpolar side chain with a charged one, which raises solubility.\nAsp → Glu: both are −1 carboxylates; only one CH₂ is added.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A13',from:'pe6-cp:49'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A lysine side chain forms a salt bridge with a glutamate in a protein\'s folded structure at pH 7.4. Which replacement for the lysine is most likely to preserve this interaction?',choices:['Methionine','Arginine','Glutamine','Aspartate'],ans:1,exp:'A salt bridge with Glu (−1) needs a **positive** side chain. **Arginine\'s guanidinium (pKa ≈ 12.5) is +1 at pH 7.4** and has a similar long side chain, so it is the conservative swap.\n\nGlutamine: polar amide, neutral, so it can H-bond but not form an ion pair.\nAspartate: −1, which would repel the glutamate.\nMethionine: nonpolar thioether, no charge.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A14',from:'pe4-cp:50'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Cysteine and methionine both contain sulfur. Which statement correctly contrasts them?',choices:['Both form disulfide bonds, because both side chains contain sulfur','Neither forms disulfide bonds, because sulfur makes them nonpolar','Only cysteine forms disulfide bonds, because only cysteine has an S–H','Only methionine forms disulfide bonds, because its sulfur lies within the chain'],ans:2,exp:'A disulfide forms when two **thiols (–SH)** are **oxidized**, each losing an H: R–SH + HS–R → R–S–S–R. Cysteine has a thiol; methionine\'s sulfur is a **thioether (C–S–C)** with no H to lose.\n\nOnly methionine: a sulfur already bonded to two carbons cannot form another S–S bond.\nBoth: sulfur alone is not enough; the S–H is required.\nNeither: cysteine clearly forms disulfides; nonpolarity is irrelevant.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A15',from:'pe1-cp:22'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'The peptide Ser–Gly–Phe–Thr–Met–Tyr–Ala is incubated with ATP and a kinase able to phosphorylate any side-chain hydroxyl group. How many residues can be phosphorylated?',choices:['2','4','5','3'],ans:3,exp:'Kinases transfer ATP\'s γ-phosphate to a side-chain **–OH**, forming a phosphoester. Side chains with –OH: **Ser, Thr and Tyr (phenol) = 3**.\n\n2: misses tyrosine, whose OH sits on an aromatic ring.\n4: adds phenylalanine, which has the ring but no OH.\n5: also adds methionine, whose sulfur carries no H.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A16',from:'pe6-cp:10'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'At pH 5.0, a residue in a binding site stacks flat against a ligand\'s aromatic ring and also carries a mostly positive side chain. Which residue fits both observations?',choices:['Phenylalanine, no ionizable side chain','Histidine, side-chain pKa 6.0','Lysine, side-chain pKa 10.5','Tyrosine, side-chain pKa 10.1'],ans:1,exp:'Two features are needed: an **aromatic ring** for stacking and a **positive charge** at pH 5.0. Histidine\'s imidazole is aromatic, and pH 5.0 is one unit below its pKa, so it is ≈**91% protonated (+)**.\n\nPhenylalanine: aromatic but never charged.\nLysine: positive at pH 5.0 but has no ring to stack.\nTyrosine: aromatic, but its phenol is neutral at pH 5.0 (it would lose H⁺ only above ≈10).',src:'cp-gap-bch:A17',from:'pe5-cp:42'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'An aspartate on a protein\'s surface is mutated to asparagine. At pH 7.4, which property of the side chain changes?',choices:['Polarity, since asparagine is a nonpolar residue','Net charge, since the amide of asparagine does not ionize','Hydrogen bonding, since asparagine cannot form hydrogen bonds','Size, since asparagine has one more carbon than aspartate'],ans:1,exp:'Aspartate\'s –COO⁻ (pKa 3.9) is **−1 at pH 7.4**. Asparagine replaces it with **–CONH₂**, a neutral amide that does not ionize at physiological pH: the **charge goes from −1 to 0**.\n\nHydrogen bonding: the amide has an N–H donor and a C=O acceptor, so it still H-bonds.\nSize: both side chains are CH₂ plus one carbonyl carbon.\nPolarity: asparagine is a polar residue.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A18',from:'pe5-cp:18'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Glycine (75 Da), alanine (89 Da) and serine (105 Da) are linked into one linear tripeptide. What is the tripeptide\'s mass?',choices:['269 Da','215 Da','233 Da','251 Da'],ans:2,exp:'Each peptide bond is a **condensation that releases one H₂O (18 Da)**. A linear tripeptide has **2 peptide bonds**.\n75 + 89 + 105 = 269; 269 − 2(18) = **233 Da**.\n\n251 Da: removes only one water, as for a dipeptide.\n269 Da: forgets that water is lost.\n215 Da: removes three waters, as for a cyclic tripeptide.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A19',from:'pe3-cp:27'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'The nitrogen of a peptide bond is neither basic nor a good hydrogen-bond acceptor. Which explanation is correct?',choices:['Its lone pair sits in an sp³ orbital held tightly by the nucleus','It is protonated at pH 7.4, so it has no free lone pair','Its lone pair is used in the covalent bond to the α-carbon','Its lone pair is delocalized into the C=O by resonance'],ans:3,exp:'In an amide, the N lone pair is in **resonance with the carbonyl**: O=C–N ↔ ⁻O–C=N⁺. The lone pair is spread toward oxygen, so it is unavailable to bind H⁺ or accept an H-bond, and the **C–N bond has partial double-bond character**; the N is sp², not sp³.\n\nsp³ orbital: the amide N is sp² and planar because of resonance.\nProtonated: amide N has a very low basicity and is not protonated at pH 7.4.\nBond to the α-carbon: that bond uses a separate N orbital, not the lone pair.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A20',from:'pe3-cp:37'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Because of resonance in the peptide bond, which backbone bond in a polypeptide has restricted rotation?',choices:['The Cα–C(O) bond, α-carbon to carbonyl','The N–Cα bond, nitrogen to α-carbon','The C(O)–N bond, carbonyl to nitrogen','The Cα–Cβ bond, α-carbon to side chain'],ans:2,exp:'Delocalizing the N lone pair into the C=O gives the **C(O)–N bond partial double-bond character**. Like a C=C, it cannot rotate without breaking π overlap, so the six atoms around it lie in **one plane**.\n\nN–Cα: a single bond; rotation about it (φ) is what lets the chain fold.\nCα–C(O): also a single bond (ψ), free to rotate.\nCα–Cβ: an ordinary single bond to the side chain.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A21',from:'pe3-cp:37'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5B',q:'Which description correctly gives the hydrogen-bonding roles of the polypeptide backbone atoms in secondary structures?',choices:['The α-carbon C–H donates and the amide N accepts','The amide N–H donates and the carbonyl O accepts','The carbonyl O donates and the amide N–H accepts','The amide N accepts with its lone pair and the C=O donates'],ans:1,exp:'A **donor** needs H on N, O or F; an **acceptor** needs a free lone pair on N, O or F. The backbone **N–H donates**; the **C=O oxygen**, with lone pairs and no H, **accepts**. Regular N–H···O=C bonds hold helices and sheets together.\n\nCarbonyl O as donor: it has no H to donate.\nAmide N accepting: its lone pair is tied up in amide resonance.\nC–H as donor: carbon is not electronegative enough to make a hydrogen bond.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A22',from:'pe6-cp:23'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Complete hydrolysis of a tetrapeptide gives equal amounts of Ala, Gly, Ser and Val. Partial hydrolysis gives the dipeptides Ala–Ser, Val–Gly and Ser–Val (each written N→C). What is the sequence, N→C?',choices:['Val–Gly–Ala–Ser','Ser–Val–Ala–Gly','Ala–Ser–Val–Gly','Gly–Val–Ser–Ala'],ans:2,exp:'Overlap the fragments by their shared residues: **Ala–Ser** + **Ser–Val** → Ala–Ser–Val; + **Val–Gly** → **Ala–Ser–Val–Gly**. Every dipeptide is present and each residue appears once.\n\nGly–Val–Ser–Ala: the right chain read C→N; it would give Gly–Val, not Val–Gly.\nVal–Gly–Ala–Ser: joins two fragments end to end and contains no Ser–Val.\nSer–Val–Ala–Gly: keeps only Ser–Val and contains neither Ala–Ser nor Val–Gly.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A23',from:'pe6-cp:25'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A pentapeptide is cleaved by two methods. Method 1 gives Gly–Leu–Met and Lys–Phe; method 2 gives Gly–Leu, Met–Lys and Phe (all written N→C). What is the pentapeptide\'s sequence, N→C?',choices:['Lys–Phe–Gly–Leu–Met','Met–Lys–Phe–Gly–Leu','Gly–Leu–Phe–Met–Lys','Gly–Leu–Met–Lys–Phe'],ans:3,exp:'Method 2\'s **Met–Lys** links the two method 1 fragments: Gly–Leu–**Met** must be followed by **Lys**–Phe → **Gly–Leu–Met–Lys–Phe**. Check method 2: Gly–Leu | Met–Lys | Phe. ✓\n\nLys–Phe–Gly–Leu–Met: fits method 1 but has no Met–Lys.\nMet–Lys–Phe–Gly–Leu: fits method 2 but not Gly–Leu–Met.\nGly–Leu–Phe–Met–Lys: fits neither, since it has no Lys–Phe or Leu–Met.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A24',from:'pe6-cp:25'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A soluble enzyme with two internal disulfide bonds is placed in 8 M urea with no reducing agent and loses all activity. Which bonds or interactions remain intact?',choices:['Hydrophobic contacts and salt bridges','Peptide bonds only','Disulfide bonds and backbone hydrogen bonds','Peptide bonds and disulfide bonds'],ans:3,exp:'Urea is a **denaturant**: it disrupts **noncovalent** forces (H-bonds, hydrophobic contacts, salt bridges) and unfolds secondary and tertiary structure. It does not break **covalent** bonds: the **peptide bonds** (primary structure) stay, and so do the **disulfides**, since no reducing agent is present.\n\nPeptide bonds only: breaking a disulfide needs a reducing agent.\nBackbone H-bonds: these noncovalent bonds are what urea disrupts.\nHydrophobic contacts and salt bridges: noncovalent, so lost on unfolding.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A25',from:'pe5-cp:33'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A purified protein is boiled in SDS and analyzed by SDS-PAGE. Without β-mercaptoethanol it gives one band at 60 kDa; with β-mercaptoethanol it gives one band at 30 kDa. Which structure best explains these results?',choices:['One 60-kDa chain cut in half at a peptide bond','Two identical 30-kDa chains joined by a disulfide bond','Two identical 30-kDa chains held only by hydrophobic contacts','One 60-kDa chain folded by internal disulfide bonds'],ans:1,exp:'SDS unfolds proteins and breaks **noncovalent** contacts between subunits; only **covalent** links survive. β-Mercaptoethanol **reduces disulfides**. Mass halves only when it is added, so the protein is **two 30-kDa chains linked by a disulfide (–S–S–)**.\n\nHydrophobic contacts only: SDS alone would separate the chains, giving 30 kDa without β-mercaptoethanol.\nInternal disulfides: reducing them changes shape, not the mass of a single chain.\nPeptide cleavage: β-mercaptoethanol reduces S–S bonds and does not hydrolyze amide bonds.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A26',from:'pe1-cp:22'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Pairs of side chains are found in contact inside folded proteins at pH 7.4. Which pair is correctly matched with the interaction that mainly holds it together?',choices:['Cys and Met: covalent disulfide bond','Phe and Lys: ionic salt bridge','Leu and Val: hydrophobic contact','Asp and Glu: ionic salt bridge'],ans:2,exp:'**Leucine and valine** are both nonpolar alkyl side chains, held together by **hydrophobic contacts** (dispersion forces plus the exclusion of water).\n\nAsp and Glu: both are −1 at pH 7.4; like charges repel, so no salt bridge.\nCys and Met: a disulfide needs two cysteine thiols; methionine has no S–H.\nPhe and Lys: phenylalanine is uncharged, so there is no ion pair.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A27',from:'pe5-cp:42'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'An enzyme is unfolded in 6 M guanidinium chloride and then diluted into buffer to let it refold. Which observation best shows that its native tertiary structure has returned?',choices:['Its mass by mass spectrometry matches the sequence','It runs as a single band on SDS-PAGE','Its amino acid composition is unchanged','Its catalytic activity returns to normal'],ans:3,exp:'Catalysis needs a correctly formed **active site**, which exists only in the **native 3-D fold**. Recovering full activity is the strongest evidence of refolding.\n\nMass: set by the covalent sequence, the same folded or unfolded.\nSingle SDS-PAGE band: SDS unfolds the protein anyway, so the band reports size and purity only.\nComposition: the denaturant does not change the residues, so it reports nothing about folding.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A28',from:'pe5-cp:25'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Thermal unfolding is followed for a wild-type protein and a point mutant. Wild type: half unfolded at 66 °C, transition from 90% to 10% folded over 8 °C. Mutant: half unfolded at 53 °C, transition over 8 °C. Which conclusion is best supported?',choices:['The mutant is more stable, since it unfolds at a lower temperature','The mutant is less stable, since its midpoint is 13 °C lower','The mutant unfolds less cooperatively, since its midpoint is lower','Both are equally stable, since both transitions span 8 °C'],ans:1,exp:'**Stability is read from the midpoint (Tm)**; the mutant\'s Tm is **13 °C lower**, so its folded state is less stable. **Cooperativity is read from the steepness**, and both span 8 °C, so cooperativity is unchanged.\n\nMore stable: inverts the meaning; unfolding at lower temperature means less stable.\nLess cooperative: a lower midpoint does not make the transition broader.\nEqually stable: the width measures cooperativity, not stability.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A29',from:'pe3-cp:42'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A water-soluble reagent that labels lysine side chains modifies 12 of an enzyme\'s 15 lysines in the native enzyme and all 15 after the enzyme is unfolded in urea. Which explanation is most consistent?',choices:['Urea converts three arginines into lysines','Three lysines are buried within the native fold','Urea adds three lysines by hydrolyzing peptide bonds','Three lysines are joined by disulfide bonds in the native fold'],ans:1,exp:'A water-soluble reagent reaches only **solvent-exposed** side chains. Unfolding exposes every residue, so the **3 extra** lysines labeled in urea must be **buried or shielded in the native tertiary structure**.\n\nHydrolysis: urea denatures without breaking peptide bonds, and hydrolysis cannot create new residues.\nDisulfides: these form only between cysteines; lysine has no thiol.\nConverting residues: urea does not change side-chain identity.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A30',from:'pe6-cp:37'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'During folding of a soluble protein, nonpolar side chains collect in the core. This burial is favorable mainly because:',choices:['the alkyl side chains form strong hydrogen bonds with each other','water around the side chains becomes more ordered','water released from ordered shells around them gains entropy','covalent bonds form between the buried alkyl side chains'],ans:2,exp:'Water forms ordered, cage-like shells around exposed nonpolar groups. Burying those groups **releases the ordered water**, raising its **entropy** (ΔS > 0) and making ΔG negative: the **hydrophobic effect**, the main force driving folding.\n\nCovalent bonds: hydrophobic contacts are noncovalent.\nHydrogen bonds: C–H groups cannot form them.\nMore ordered water: describes the unfavorable exposed state, the opposite of burial.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A31',from:'pe4-cp:36'},
  {s:'Amino Acids & Proteins',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A purified enzyme is incubated in 8 M urea with no reducing agent, and its activity drops to zero. When the urea is removed by slow dialysis, the enzyme regains nearly all of its activity with no other additions. Which conclusion is best supported?',choices:['Urea hydrolyzed peptide bonds, and dialysis later rejoined them','Urea broke noncovalent bonds; the sequence guides refolding','Urea reduced the disulfide bonds that alone held the active fold','Urea stripped off a bound cofactor, and dialysis returned it'],ans:1,exp:'Urea is a **denaturant**: it disrupts the hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic contacts that hold **secondary and tertiary structure**, so the chain unfolds and activity is lost. Peptide bonds are untouched, so the **primary structure** survives; once the urea is gone, the chain folds back into its active shape on its own, showing that the **sequence encodes the fold**.\n\nHydrolyzed peptide bonds: urea does not cut the backbone, and dialysis could not re-form covalent peptide bonds.\nReduced disulfides: urea is not a reducing agent, and none was added; a lost fold that returns points to noncovalent forces.\nStripped cofactor: dialysis removes small molecules, so it could not hand a lost cofactor back.',src:'cp-gap-bch:A32',from:'pe5-cp:33'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A hydrolase is assayed at several substrate concentrations, and its rate levels off near 80 μM/min at very high [S]. Initial rates: [S] = 5 μM, v = 40 μM/min; [S] = 15 μM, v = 60 μM/min; [S] = 45 μM, v = 72 μM/min; [S] = 95 μM, v = 76 μM/min. What is Km?',choices:['2.5 μM','5 μM','15 μM','40 μM'],ans:1,exp:'**Km is the [S] that gives half of Vmax.** Vmax ≈ 80 μM/min, so half is 40 μM/min, reached at **[S] = 5 μM**.\nCheck: v = 80 × 15 / (5 + 15) = 60 ✓.\n\n2.5 μM halves the Km itself instead of Vmax.\n15 μM is the [S] that gives 3/4 of Vmax, not 1/2.\n40 μM copies the half-Vmax rate value and gives it concentration units.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B1',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A purified dehydrogenase is used at a total concentration of 0.02 μM, and its Vmax under these conditions is 6 μM/s. What is kcat?',choices:['0.12 s⁻¹','30 s⁻¹','300 s⁻¹','3000 s⁻¹'],ans:2,exp:'**kcat = Vmax / [E]total** = (6 μM/s) / (0.02 μM) = **300 s⁻¹**: each active site turns over 300 substrate molecules per second.\n\n0.12 s⁻¹ multiplies Vmax by [E] instead of dividing.\n30 s⁻¹ and 3000 s⁻¹ are decimal slips of 10× in dividing by 0.02.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B2',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'One protease cleaves two peptide substrates. Substrate X: kcat = 300 s⁻¹, Km = 60 μM. Substrate Y: kcat = 45 s⁻¹, Km = 3 μM. Both are mixed with the enzyme at equal concentrations far below either Km. What is the ratio of the rate of cleavage of Y to that of X?',choices:['20','0.15','3','0.33'],ans:2,exp:'At [S] far below Km, **v ≈ (kcat/Km)[E][S]**. Both substrates share the same enzyme and the same concentration, so the rate ratio equals the ratio of the **specificity constants**, even while they compete.\nX: 300 ÷ 60 = **5 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**. Y: 45 ÷ 3 = **15 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**. vY/vX = 15 ÷ 5 = **3**.\n\n0.15: the ratio of kcat values alone (45/300), which sets the rate only at saturating [S].\n20: the ratio of Km values alone (60/3), which ignores how fast each complex turns over.\n0.33: the ratio of X to Y, inverted.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B3',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Two isozymes act on glucose. Isozyme A has Km = 0.1 mM and isozyme B has Km = 10 mM. At a glucose concentration of 5 mM, about what fraction of its own Vmax does each isozyme reach?',choices:['A about 98%; B about 50%','A about 50%; B about 50%','A about 33%; B about 98%','A about 98%; B about 33%'],ans:3,exp:'**v/Vmax = [S] / (Km + [S]).**\nA: 5 / (0.1 + 5) = 5 / 5.1 ≈ **0.98**, nearly saturated.\nB: 5 / (10 + 5) = 5 / 15 ≈ **0.33**.\nA low Km means the enzyme is half-saturated at low [S].\n\n50% for both assumes [S] equals Km for each, which it does not.\nSwapping the values treats a high Km as tight binding.\nB at 50% would need [S] = 10 mM, its Km.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B4',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An enzyme has Vmax = 120 μM/min and Km = 4 mM. What initial rate is expected at [S] = 12 mM?',choices:['30 μM/min','60 μM/min','90 μM/min','120 μM/min'],ans:2,exp:'**v = Vmax[S] / (Km + [S])** = 120 × 12 / (4 + 12) = 1440 / 16 = **90 μM/min**, three-quarters of Vmax since [S] = 3Km.\n\n30 μM/min uses Km/(Km + [S]) = 1/4 instead of [S]/(Km + [S]).\n60 μM/min is the rate at [S] = Km, not at 3Km.\n120 μM/min assumes saturation, which needs [S] much larger than Km.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B5',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Product formed by an enzyme at a fixed starting [S] was measured over time: 0 min, 0 μM; 1 min, 12 μM; 2 min, 24 μM; 5 min, 52 μM; 10 min, 80 μM; 30 min, 100 μM. Which rate belongs on a Michaelis–Menten plot for this [S]?',choices:['About 10 μM/min','About 12 μM/min','About 8 μM/min','About 3.3 μM/min'],ans:1,exp:'Michaelis–Menten analysis uses the **initial rate v₀**, taken while the product rises linearly and [S] is still the stated value. Over the first 2 min the product climbs 12 μM each minute: **v₀ ≈ 12 μM/min**.\n\n10 μM/min (52/5) averages in the slowing after 2 min.\n8 μM/min (80/10) averages over a stretch where substrate is being used up.\n3.3 μM/min (100/30) is the average over the whole run, near the end point.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B6',from:'pe3-cp:28'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A Lineweaver–Burk plot of 1/v against 1/[S] for an enzyme gives a straight line that crosses the y-axis at 0.05 min/μM and the x-axis at −0.25 μM⁻¹. What are Vmax and Km?',choices:['Vmax 20 μM/min; Km 0.25 μM','Vmax 4 μM/min; Km 20 μM','Vmax 20 μM/min; Km 4 μM','Vmax 0.05 μM/min; Km 4 μM'],ans:2,exp:'**y-intercept = 1/Vmax**, so Vmax = 1 / 0.05 = **20 μM/min**.\n**x-intercept = −1/Km**, so Km = 1 / 0.25 = **4 μM**.\n\nSwapping the two values pairs each intercept with the wrong constant.\nKm 0.25 μM reads the x-intercept without taking the reciprocal.\nVmax 0.05 μM/min reads the y-intercept without taking the reciprocal.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B7',from:'pe4-cp:34'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'In a double-reciprocal plot for an enzyme, the line has a y-intercept of 0.02 s/μM and a slope of 0.1 s. What is Km?',choices:['0.002 μM','0.2 μM','5 μM','50 μM'],ans:2,exp:'**Slope = Km/Vmax** and **y-intercept = 1/Vmax**.\nVmax = 1 / 0.02 = 50 μM/s, so Km = slope × Vmax = 0.1 s × 50 μM/s = **5 μM**.\n(Equivalently, slope ÷ intercept = Km.)\n\n0.002 μM multiplies slope by intercept.\n0.2 μM divides intercept by slope, which gives 1/Km.\n50 μM is Vmax with its units changed.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B8',from:'pe4-cp:34'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Double-reciprocal plots of an enzyme with and without inhibitor P both cross the y-axis at 0.1 s/μM. Without P the x-intercept is −0.5 μM⁻¹; with P it is −0.125 μM⁻¹. Which describes inhibitor P?',choices:['Noncompetitive; Vmax falls from 10 to 2.5 μM/s','Competitive; Km falls from 8 to 2 μM','Uncompetitive; Km falls from 8 to 2 μM','Competitive; Km rises from 2 to 8 μM'],ans:3,exp:'Same y-intercept means **Vmax is unchanged** (10 μM/s); the lines meet on the y-axis, the signature of **competitive** inhibition.\nKm = −1/x-intercept: 1/0.5 = **2 μM** without P and 1/0.125 = **8 μM** with P, so the apparent Km rises.\n\nKm falling reverses which intercept belongs to which condition.\nUncompetitive inhibition gives parallel lines, not lines meeting on the y-axis.\nNoncompetitive inhibition would raise the y-intercept; here it does not move.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B9',from:'pe6-cp:15'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'With and without compound R, an enzyme gives two parallel lines on a Lineweaver–Burk plot. Without R the line crosses the y-axis at 0.05 and the x-axis at −0.2; with R it crosses at 0.10 and −0.4 (μM, min units). What does R do?',choices:['Lowers Vmax to 10 μM/min; Km stays at 5 μM','Lowers Vmax to 10 μM/min and Km to 2.5 μM','Raises Km to 10 μM; Vmax stays at 20 μM/min','Lowers Vmax to 10 μM/min; raises Km to 10 μM'],ans:1,exp:'y-intercepts: 1/0.05 = 20 → 1/0.10 = **10 μM/min**.\nx-intercepts: 1/0.2 = 5 → 1/0.4 = **2.5 μM**.\nBoth fall by the same factor, so the slope Km/Vmax stays 0.25 and the lines are **parallel**: **uncompetitive** inhibition, where the inhibitor binds only ES.\n\nKm unchanged with lower Vmax is pure noncompetitive; those lines meet on the x-axis.\nKm up with Vmax unchanged is competitive; those lines meet on the y-axis.\nKm 10 μM takes the reciprocal of the new y-intercept (1/0.10) instead of the x-intercept.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B10',from:'pe4-cp:34'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An inhibitor changes an enzyme\'s kinetic constants from Km = 3 mM, Vmax = 90 μM/min to Km = 3 mM, Vmax = 30 μM/min. Where do the Lineweaver–Burk lines with and without inhibitor intersect?',choices:['Nowhere, since the lines are parallel','On the y-axis, at 0.011 min/μM','On the x-axis, at −0.33 mM⁻¹','Left of the y-axis, above the x-axis'],ans:2,exp:'Km is unchanged, so both lines share the x-intercept **−1/Km = −1/3 ≈ −0.33 mM⁻¹**; only the y-intercept (1/Vmax) rises, from 1/90 to 1/30. Lines meeting on the x-axis mark **pure noncompetitive** inhibition.\n\nMeeting on the y-axis (at 1/90) would require an unchanged Vmax: competitive.\nParallel lines require Km and Vmax to fall by the same factor: uncompetitive.\nMeeting left of the y-axis above the x-axis requires Km to change too: mixed.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B11',from:'pe6-cp:15'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Kinetic constants for an enzyme: no inhibitor, Km = 4 μM and Vmax = 60 μM/s; with inhibitor Q, Km = 12 μM and Vmax = 30 μM/s. Which type of inhibition does Q show?',choices:['Competitive','Uncompetitive','Pure noncompetitive','Mixed'],ans:3,exp:'Q **lowers Vmax** (60 → 30) **and changes Km** (4 → 12, up 3×, while Vmax falls only 2×). A drop in Vmax plus a Km change that does not match it is **mixed** inhibition: Q binds both E and ES, with different affinities.\n\nCompetitive would leave Vmax at 60.\nUncompetitive would lower Km by the same factor as Vmax (to 2 μM).\nPure noncompetitive would leave Km at 4 μM.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B12',from:'pe4-cp:34'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'With inhibitor W present, an enzyme\'s Km falls from 6 μM to 2 μM and its Vmax falls from 90 to 30 nM/s. Where does W most likely bind?',choices:['To the substrate, lowering its free concentration','Only to the free enzyme, at the active site','To free enzyme and ES complex equally well','Only to the enzyme–substrate complex'],ans:3,exp:'Km and Vmax both drop **threefold**, the pattern of **uncompetitive** inhibition. The inhibitor binds **only ES**; pulling ES out of solution makes the enzyme appear to bind substrate more tightly (lower apparent Km) while trapping it in a dead-end complex (lower Vmax).\n\nBinding only free enzyme at the active site is competitive: Km up, Vmax unchanged.\nEqual binding to E and ES is pure noncompetitive: Km unchanged.\nTying up substrate would act like lowering [S]; it could not lower Km.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B13',from:'pe6-cp:15'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An enzyme with Vmax = 50 μM/min is assayed with a fixed concentration of a competitive inhibitor. As [S] is raised to many thousand times Km, the initial rate approaches about:',choices:['25 μM/min, since the inhibitor occupies half of the sites','50 μM/min, since excess substrate outcompetes the inhibitor','0 μM/min, since the inhibitor binds the active site for good','a value below 50 μM/min set by the inhibitor concentration'],ans:1,exp:'A competitive inhibitor and the substrate bind the **same site** reversibly and in a mutually exclusive way. At very high [S], substrate wins nearly every site, so the rate approaches the **unchanged Vmax, 50 μM/min**. Only the apparent Km rises.\n\n25 μM/min assumes a fixed fraction of sites stays blocked, as in noncompetitive inhibition.\n0 μM/min treats the inhibitor as irreversible; a competitive inhibitor comes off and can be displaced by substrate.\nA cap set by [I] describes noncompetitive or mixed inhibition, which lowers Vmax.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B14',from:'pe5-cp:4'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An enzyme has Km = 2 μM. A competitive inhibitor with Ki = 10 μM is added at 30 μM. Apparent Km = Km(1 + [I]/Ki). What is the apparent Km?',choices:['2 μM','6 μM','8 μM','32 μM'],ans:2,exp:'[I]/Ki = 30/10 = 3, so apparent Km = 2 μM × (1 + 3) = **8 μM**.\n\n2 μM leaves Km unchanged, as for a noncompetitive inhibitor.\n6 μM multiplies by [I]/Ki and drops the 1.\n32 μM adds [I] to Km, mixing up two different quantities.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B15',from:'pe5-cp:4'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Three inhibitors of the same enzyme have these Ki values: A, 50 nM; B, 2 μM; C, 0.5 μM. Which inhibitor binds most tightly, and how does it compare with C?',choices:['B; it binds about 4 times more tightly than C','A; it binds about 100 times more tightly than C','A; it binds about 10 times more tightly than C','C; it binds about 10 times more tightly than A'],ans:2,exp:'**Ki is a dissociation constant: the lower it is, the tighter the binding.** Convert to one unit: A = 50 nM, C = 0.5 μM = 500 nM, B = 2000 nM. A is lowest, and 500/50 = **10×** tighter than C.\n\n100× uses 0.5 μM = 5000 nM, a unit slip.\nB has the largest Ki, so it binds most weakly.\nC beating A treats a larger Ki as tighter binding.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B16',from:'pe5-cp:39'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An inhibitor binds a free enzyme with Ki = 4 μM. With no substrate present, the inhibitor is added at 12 μM, far above the enzyme concentration. What fraction of the enzyme is present as EI at equilibrium?',choices:['25%','33%','50%','75%'],ans:3,exp:'For E + I ⇌ EI with Ki = [E][I]/[EI], the fraction bound is **[I] / (Ki + [I])** = 12 / (4 + 12) = **0.75**.\nThe ratio [EI]/[E] = [I]/Ki = 3, so 3 of every 4 enzymes are bound.\n\n25% is the fraction still free, Ki/(Ki + [I]).\n33% is Ki/[I], an inverted ratio.\n50% is the fraction bound only when [I] equals Ki.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B17',from:'pe5-cp:4'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Hill coefficients (n) are measured for oxygen binding by three proteins: P, n = 2.8; Q, n = 1.0; R, n = 0.6. Which statement is supported?',choices:['P binds noncooperatively and gives a hyperbolic curve','Q binds cooperatively and gives a sigmoidal curve','R binds cooperatively and gives a sigmoidal curve','P binds cooperatively and gives a sigmoidal curve'],ans:3,exp:'**n > 1: positive cooperativity**, a sigmoidal curve (P, like hemoglobin).\n**n = 1: no cooperativity**, a hyperbola (Q, like myoglobin).\n**n < 1: negative cooperativity** (R): each binding makes the next weaker, and the curve is flatter than a hyperbola.\n\nQ, with n = 1, is the noncooperative reference.\nR has n below 1, the opposite of sigmoidal binding.\nA hyperbola goes with n = 1, not 2.8.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B18',from:'pe5-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A ligand-binding protein of unknown subunit count gives a sigmoidal binding curve with a Hill coefficient of 3.0. Which conclusion is best supported?',choices:['Binding is positively cooperative, and there are exactly 3 sites','Binding is positively cooperative, and there are at least 3 sites','Binding is negatively cooperative, and there are at least 3 sites','Binding is noncooperative, and there are exactly 3 sites'],ans:1,exp:'n > 1 means **positive cooperativity**. The Hill coefficient **cannot exceed the number of interacting sites** (it equals it only for perfect, all-or-none cooperativity), so n = 3 means **at least 3** sites. Hemoglobin, with 4 sites, has n ≈ 2.8.\n\n"Exactly 3" treats n as a site count, which it is not.\nNegative cooperativity gives n < 1 and a flattened curve.\nNoncooperative binding gives n = 1 and a hyperbola.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B19',from:'pe5-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Two carrier proteins are both half-saturated at [L] = 10 μM. Cooperative protein K is about 0.1 saturated at 5 μM and 0.8 at 15 μM. Noncooperative protein N is about 0.33 saturated at 5 μM and 0.6 at 15 μM. When [L] falls from 15 to 5 μM, which protein releases more ligand per site?',choices:['K, by about 8 times','N, by about 2.5 times','K, by about 2.5 times','Neither, since both have the same half-saturation'],ans:2,exp:'Release = drop in fractional saturation.\nK: 0.8 − 0.1 = **0.7**. N: 0.6 − 0.33 ≈ **0.27**.\n0.7 / 0.27 ≈ **2.5**: the steep middle of a **sigmoidal** curve lets a cooperative carrier unload far more over a small range of [L], as hemoglobin does.\n\nN releasing more swaps the two curves.\n8 times divides K\'s high value by its low value (0.8/0.1) instead of comparing the two proteins.\nA shared half-saturation point says nothing about how steeply each curve changes around it.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B20',from:'pe5-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An enzyme is assayed at two concentrations. At 10 nM enzyme: Vmax = 4 μM/s, Km = 3 μM. At 20 nM enzyme: Vmax = 8 μM/s, Km = 3 μM. Which conclusion is supported?',choices:['Affinity for substrate doubles with more enzyme','kcat doubles from 400 to 800 s⁻¹ with more enzyme','kcat is 0.4 s⁻¹ at both enzyme concentrations','kcat is 400 s⁻¹ at both enzyme concentrations'],ans:3,exp:'kcat = Vmax/[E]: 4 μM/s ÷ 0.010 μM = **400 s⁻¹**, and 8 ÷ 0.020 = **400 s⁻¹**. Vmax scales with enzyme amount, but **kcat and Km are properties of each enzyme molecule** and do not change.\n\nkcat doubling confuses Vmax with kcat.\n0.4 s⁻¹ divides by 10 instead of 0.010 μM, a nM-to-μM slip.\nKm is unchanged at 3 μM, so affinity did not change.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B21',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A Michaelis–Menten curve for 2 μM enzyme levels off at 0.6 mM/s, and the rate is 0.3 mM/s when [S] = 6 mM. What is kcat/Km?',choices:['25 mM⁻¹s⁻¹','50 mM⁻¹s⁻¹','1800 mM⁻¹s⁻¹','0.05 mM⁻¹s⁻¹'],ans:1,exp:'Km = [S] at half of Vmax = **6 mM** (0.3 is half of 0.6).\nkcat = Vmax/[E] = 0.6 mM/s ÷ 0.002 mM = **300 s⁻¹**.\nkcat/Km = 300 / 6 = **50 mM⁻¹s⁻¹** (5 × 10⁴ M⁻¹s⁻¹).\n\n25 uses the half-maximal rate, 0.3 mM/s, as Vmax.\n1800 multiplies kcat by Km instead of dividing.\n0.05 divides by 2 instead of 0.002 mM, a μM-to-mM slip.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B22',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Carbonic anhydrase has kcat ≈ 1 × 10⁶ s⁻¹. At saturating CO₂, about how long does one active site take to complete a single catalytic cycle?',choices:['1 ms','1 μs','1 ns','1 s'],ans:1,exp:'**kcat is the turnover number**: reactions per active site per second at saturation. Time per cycle = 1/kcat = 1 / (10⁶ s⁻¹) = **10⁻⁶ s = 1 μs**.\n\n1 ms is 1/(10³ s⁻¹), off by a factor of 1000.\n1 ns would need kcat = 10⁹ s⁻¹.\n1 s confuses kcat with a rate of one turnover per second.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B23',from:'pe4-cp:43'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Sucrase converts sucrose and one molecule of water into glucose and fructose; no ATP or redox cofactor is involved. To which enzyme class does sucrase belong?',choices:['Transferase, since a sugar is moved to water','Lyase, since one molecule is split into two','Hydrolase, since water is used to split a bond','Isomerase, since glucose and fructose are isomers'],ans:2,exp:'Water is a **reactant** and a glycosidic bond is cleaved, so sucrase is a **hydrolase**.\n\nLyases split bonds **without water** (often leaving a double bond or ring).\nNaming water as the acceptor of a sugar is a hydrolysis described backwards; transferases move groups between two substrates.\nThe products are isomers of each other, but an isomerase converts one molecule into its own isomer.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B24',from:'pe3-cp:22'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Aldolase splits fructose-1,6-bisphosphate, a six-carbon sugar, into two three-carbon phosphorylated sugars. No water, ATP or redox cofactor is used, and the reaction is reversible. Which enzyme class fits?',choices:['Hydrolase','Lyase','Ligase','Oxidoreductase'],ans:1,exp:'A C–C bond is broken **without water and without redox**: that is a **lyase**. (Run in reverse, a lyase joins two molecules without ATP.)\n\nHydrolases need water as a reactant; none is used.\nLigases join two molecules using ATP; here a molecule is split and no ATP is used.\nOxidoreductases transfer electrons; no cofactor is reduced or oxidized.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B25',from:'pe3-cp:22'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Glutamine synthetase joins glutamate and NH₄⁺ to form glutamine, and the reaction consumes one ATP, which becomes ADP + Pi. To which enzyme class does it belong?',choices:['Hydrolase, since ATP is split by water','Transferase, since a phosphate leaves ATP','Lyase, since two molecules become one','Ligase, since ATP powers the joining'],ans:3,exp:'The defining event is **joining two molecules, glutamate and NH₄⁺, powered by ATP**: a **ligase** (a synthetase).\n\nATP cleavage is the energy source, not the purpose of the enzyme, so it is not a hydrolase.\nThe phosphate is only a transient intermediate; no group ends up on a second substrate.\nLyases join molecules without ATP (synthases), typically by adding across a double bond.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B26',from:'pe3-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Lactate dehydrogenase converts pyruvate + NADH + H⁺ into lactate + NAD⁺, turning a ketone C=O into a CH–OH. Which describes the enzyme and its substrate?',choices:['Lyase; pyruvate is oxidized','Oxidoreductase; pyruvate is oxidized','Transferase; pyruvate is reduced','Oxidoreductase; pyruvate is reduced'],ans:3,exp:'NADH gives up a hydride (2 e⁻) and becomes NAD⁺, so electrons move to pyruvate: the enzyme is an **oxidoreductase** and pyruvate is **reduced** (C=O → CH–OH, carbon gains a bond to H).\n\nPyruvate oxidized has the electron flow backwards; NADH is the reduced cofactor.\nA hydride carried from NADH is a redox step, so the class is oxidoreductase, not transferase.\nNo bond is split without water; this is not a lyase.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B27',from:'pe3-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Phosphoglucomutase converts glucose-6-phosphate (C₆H₁₃O₉P) into glucose-1-phosphate (C₆H₁₃O₉P), moving the phosphate from C6 to C1 of the same sugar. Which enzyme class fits?',choices:['Transferase, since a phosphate group is moved','Isomerase, since one molecule is rearranged','Hydrolase, since a phosphoester bond is broken','Ligase, since a new phosphoester bond is formed'],ans:1,exp:'Same formula in and out, one substrate and one product: the enzyme rearranges a molecule into its **isomer**, so it is an **isomerase** (a mutase moves a group within one molecule).\n\nTransferases move a group **between two molecules**.\nHydrolysis would add water and release free phosphate, changing the formula.\nLigases join two molecules using ATP; no ATP is used.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B28',from:'pe3-cp:22'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Hexokinase converts glucose + ATP into glucose-6-phosphate + ADP. To which enzyme class does hexokinase belong?',choices:['Ligase, since one ATP is consumed in the reaction','Hydrolase, since ATP is split into ADP and a phosphate','Transferase, since glucose gains a phosphoryl group','Isomerase, since glucose is converted into a new sugar'],ans:2,exp:'The γ-phosphoryl group of ATP is moved onto glucose: a group carried **from one molecule to another** makes a kinase a **transferase**.\n\nLigases use ATP only as energy to join two molecules; here the phosphoryl group itself ends up in the product.\nHydrolysis would hand the phosphate to water, not to glucose.\nAn isomerase works on one molecule; here two substrates react.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B29',from:'pe3-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Four enzymes catalyze these reactions: (1) A–B + H₂O → A–OH + B–H; (2) A + B + ATP → A–B + ADP + Pi; (3) H–X–Y–OH → X=Y + H₂O; (4) A–X + B → A + B–X. Which are the hydrolase and the ligase, respectively?',choices:['3 and 2','1 and 2','1 and 4','4 and 2'],ans:1,exp:'**Hydrolase**: water is a reactant that splits a bond, reaction **1**.\n**Ligase**: two molecules joined with ATP, reaction **2**.\nReaction 3 removes water to form a double bond (a lyase, e.g. a dehydratase); reaction 4 moves group X between molecules (a transferase).\n\n3 and 2 confuses losing water (lyase) with using water.\n1 and 4 takes the group transfer for a joining reaction; no ATP is used.\n4 and 2 takes the transferase for the hydrolase.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B30',from:'pe3-cp:40'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Malate dehydrogenase oxidizes malate to oxaloacetate, and NAD⁺ is the cofactor. What happens to the cofactor?',choices:['NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH by gaining one electron','NADH is oxidized to NAD⁺ by losing a hydride ion','NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH by gaining a hydride ion','NAD⁺ is oxidized to NADH by gaining a proton'],ans:2,exp:'If malate is **oxidized**, the cofactor must be **reduced**. NAD⁺ accepts **a hydride (H⁻: one H⁺ and 2 e⁻)**, becoming NADH; the second H from the substrate leaves as H⁺.\n\nNADH → NAD⁺ is the reverse direction, used when a substrate is reduced.\nNAD⁺ carries two electrons at once, not one.\nGaining electrons is reduction, and a proton alone carries no electrons.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B31',from:'pe5-cp:5'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'In anaerobic bacteria, fumarate reductase converts fumarate (C₄H₂O₄²⁻) into succinate (C₄H₄O₄²⁻), saturating its C=C bond, with a tightly bound flavin that the electron-transport chain then reloads. Which describes the flavin in this step?',choices:['Oxidizing agent; FADH₂ is oxidized to FAD','Oxidizing agent; FAD is reduced to FADH₂','Reducing agent; FAD is reduced to FADH₂','Reducing agent; FADH₂ is oxidized to FAD'],ans:3,exp:'Fumarate **gains two H atoms** (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻) to become succinate, so it is **reduced**. The flavin supplies them, so it must start loaded as **FADH₂** and end as **FAD**: it is **oxidized**, and a species oxidized while reducing another is the **reducing agent**. The chain then reloads FAD to FADH₂.\n\nOxidizing agent, FAD → FADH₂: the succinate dehydrogenase direction, in which the substrate loses H.\nReducing agent, FAD → FADH₂: gaining H is reduction, and a reducing agent is itself oxidized.\nOxidizing agent, FADH₂ → FAD: the right flavin change, but the species that is oxidized acts as the reducing agent.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B32',from:'pe3-cp:24'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Dihydrolipoamide dehydrogenase holds FAD tightly. Its bound FAD takes two hydrogen atoms from a dithiol substrate, then hands them on to NAD⁺, which leaves as NADH. Over thousands of cycles, which species are used up and which is regenerated?',choices:['Dithiol and FAD used up; NAD⁺ regenerated','Dithiol and NAD⁺ used up; FAD regenerated','FAD and NAD⁺ used up; dithiol regenerated','Dithiol only used up; FAD and NAD⁺ regenerated'],ans:1,exp:'Follow the cofactor around the cycle: FAD → FADH₂ (accepts H from the dithiol) → FAD (gives H to NAD⁺). The bound flavin **returns to its starting state**, acting as part of the catalyst. The **dithiol is oxidized** and **NAD⁺ is reduced to NADH**, so both are consumed like ordinary substrates; net: dithiol + NAD⁺ → disulfide + NADH + H⁺.\n\nFAD used up ignores that each FADH₂ is reoxidized by NAD⁺.\nThe dithiol is the electron source; it leaves oxidized, not regenerated.\nNAD⁺ leaves as NADH and is not restored within this enzyme.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B33',from:'pe3-cp:29'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Initial rates (μM/s) at three substrate concentrations, without and with inhibitor J: [S] = 4 μM, 50 and 25; [S] = 12 μM, 75 and 50; [S] = 396 μM, 99 and 97. Which type of inhibition fits these data?',choices:['Uncompetitive','Pure noncompetitive','Competitive','Mixed'],ans:2,exp:'J slows the enzyme strongly at low [S] (rate halved at 4 μM), but at 396 μM the rates are nearly equal (99 vs 97): **high [S] overcomes J**, so Vmax is unchanged and only Km rises (4 → 12 μM, since v = 50 = Vmax/2 at 12 μM with J). That is **competitive** inhibition.\n\nPure noncompetitive would cut every rate by the same fraction, even at 396 μM.\nUncompetitive lowers Vmax, so the high-[S] rate with J would stay well below 99.\nMixed also lowers Vmax; it cannot be overcome by substrate.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B34',from:'pe4-cp:34'},
  {s:'Enzymes & Kinetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Without inhibitor, an enzyme gives rates of 50, 75 and 99 μM/s at [S] = 4, 12 and 396 μM, so Vmax ≈ 100 μM/s and Km = 4 μM. With inhibitor M, the rates at the same [S] are 25, 37.5 and 49.5 μM/s. What are Vmax and Km with M?',choices:['Vmax 50 μM/s; Km 8 μM','Vmax 100 μM/s; Km 12 μM','Vmax 50 μM/s; Km 2 μM','Vmax 50 μM/s; Km 4 μM'],ans:3,exp:'Every rate is cut **exactly in half**, at low and high [S] alike, so Vmax falls to **50 μM/s**. Half of the new Vmax, 25 μM/s, is reached at **[S] = 4 μM**, so Km is unchanged: **pure noncompetitive** inhibition.\n\nVmax 100 with Km 12 is the competitive pattern, where high [S] restores the rate.\nKm 2 μM halves Km along with Vmax, the uncompetitive pattern.\nKm 8 μM doubles Km as if the inhibitor competed with substrate.',src:'cp-gap-bch:B35',from:'pe6-cp:15'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A chemist dissolves crystalline D-glucose and isolates two cyclic six-membered-ring forms. In form 1 the C1 hydroxyl lies on the opposite face of the ring from C6; in form 2 it lies on the same face as C6. Everything else is identical. Form 1 is best described as:',choices:['The β anomer','The α anomer','The L enantiomer','The open-chain aldehyde'],ans:1,exp:'C1 is the **anomeric carbon**: it is bonded to the ring O and to an –OH, and it is the new stereocenter made when the ring closes. In a D-sugar, the anomeric –OH **opposite the CH₂OH (C6) is α**; on the **same face it is β**. Form 1 is α, form 2 is β, and they are anomers (diastereomers that differ only at C1).\n\nThe β anomer: that is form 2, with C1–OH on the C6 face.\nThe L enantiomer: D/L is set by C5, which is unchanged; an enantiomer would invert every stereocenter.\nThe open-chain aldehyde: both forms are rings, so C1 is not a free aldehyde.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C1',from:'pe1-cp:39'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'The debranching enzyme of glycogen hydrolyzes only the bond that joins the first glucose of a side chain to a glucose in the main chain. Which bond does it hydrolyze?',choices:['β-1,4-glycosidic','α-1,4-glycosidic','α-1,6-glycosidic','β-1,6-glycosidic'],ans:2,exp:'Glycogen chains are **α-1,4** links (C1 of one glucose to C4 of the next). A branch starts where C1 of a new chain bonds to **C6** of a main-chain residue, still with the α configuration at C1: an **α-1,6** link, roughly every 8–12 residues.\n\nα-1,4-glycosidic: this is the linear backbone bond, not the branch point.\nβ-1,4-glycosidic: this is cellulose\'s link; glycogen has no β bonds.\nβ-1,6-glycosidic: right carbon, wrong anomer; glycogen\'s anomeric links are all α.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C2',from:'pe1-cp:39'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Glycogen has an α-1,6 branch about every 10 glucose residues; amylopectin, about every 25. Four glucose polymers are hydrolyzed and their linkages quantified:\nPolymer P: 91% α-1,4, 9% α-1,6\nPolymer Q: 96% α-1,4, 4% α-1,6\nPolymer R: 100% α-1,4\nPolymer S: 100% β-1,4\nWhich polymer has the structure of glycogen?',choices:['P','Q','R','S'],ans:0,exp:'One branch per ~10 residues means about **1 linkage in 10, roughly 10%, is α-1,6**, with the rest α-1,4. Polymer P (9% α-1,6) fits glycogen, the highly branched α-glucan.\n\nPolymer Q: 4% is one branch per 25 residues, the pattern given for amylopectin (plant starch).\nPolymer R: no branches at all, as in amylose.\nPolymer S: β-1,4 links make cellulose, a straight structural polymer.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C3',from:'pe1-cp:39'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A purified intestinal enzyme is tested on three glucose disaccharides for 1 hour:\nGlc(α1→4)Glc: 95% hydrolyzed\nGlc(β1→4)Glc: < 1% hydrolyzed\nGlc(α1→6)Glc: < 1% hydrolyzed\nWhich bonds does the enzyme hydrolyze?',choices:['Any bond between two glucoses','Any 1,4 glycosidic bond','Any α glycosidic bond','Only α-1,4 glycosidic bonds'],ans:3,exp:'Compare pairs that differ in one feature. α1→4 vs β1→4 differ **only in the anomeric configuration**: only α is cut. α1→4 vs α1→6 differ **only in the acceptor carbon**: only C4 is cut. So the enzyme needs both: **α and 1,4**.\n\nAny 1,4 glycosidic bond: the β1→4 disaccharide (cellobiose) was not cut.\nAny α glycosidic bond: the α1→6 disaccharide (isomaltose) was not cut.\nAny bond between two glucoses: two of the three glucose–glucose bonds survived.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C4',from:'pe1-cp:39'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Amylose and cellulose are both unbranched chains of D-glucose joined C1 to C4. Human salivary amylase digests amylose but not cellulose. Which structural difference accounts for this?',choices:['The carbon of the acceptor glucose','The anomeric configuration at each C1','The identity of the monosaccharide','The number of branch points per chain'],ans:1,exp:'Both polymers are **1,4-linked glucose**, so the only difference is at the anomeric carbon: amylose is **α-1,4** and cellulose is **β-1,4**. Enzymes are stereospecific, and amylase\'s active site fits only the α linkage.\n\nThe carbon of the acceptor glucose: it is C4 in both polymers.\nThe identity of the monosaccharide: both are made only of glucose.\nThe number of branch points: the stem says both chains are unbranched.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C5',from:'pe1-cp:39'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A sample of 0.10 mmol of a pure triacylglycerol is incubated with a microbial lipase until no further change occurs. Neutralizing the fatty acids released requires 0.30 mmol of NaOH. Which glycerol-derived product remains?',choices:['A 1,2-diacylglycerol','A 2-monoacylglycerol','Free glycerol','Unreacted triacylglycerol'],ans:2,exp:'Each ester hydrolyzed releases **one carboxylic acid**, which uses one NaOH. 0.30 mmol NaOH ÷ 0.10 mmol triacylglycerol = **3 acids per molecule**, so all three esters were cut, leaving **glycerol** plus three fatty acids.\n\nA 2-monoacylglycerol: two esters cut, which would need only 0.20 mmol NaOH.\nA 1,2-diacylglycerol: one ester cut, 0.10 mmol NaOH.\nUnreacted triacylglycerol: no acid released, so no NaOH needed.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C6',from:'pe1-cp:34'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Lipid droplets in fat cells contain almost no water, while glycogen granules carry about twice their mass in water. Which feature of triacylglycerols best explains why fat is stored without water?',choices:['Their ester bonds hydrolyze on contact with water','Their fatty acids are all fully saturated','Their phosphate head group repels water','Their glycerol –OH groups are all esterified'],ans:3,exp:'A triacylglycerol is glycerol with **all three –OH groups esterified** to fatty acids. Nothing is left to hydrogen bond or carry a charge, so the molecule is **essentially nonpolar** and coalesces into anhydrous droplets. Glycogen\'s many –OH groups bind water.\n\nAll fully saturated: stored fats contain many unsaturated chains; saturation affects melting point, not hydration.\nPhosphate head group: that is a phospholipid; triacylglycerols have no phosphate.\nEster bonds hydrolyze on contact with water: hydrolysis needs a lipase; it does not exclude water.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C7',from:'pe6-cp:44'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Each lipid is dispersed alone in water:\nLipid 1: phosphocholine head, two C16 acyl chains\nLipid 2: phosphocholine head, one C16 acyl chain\nLipid 3: sulfate head, one C12 chain\nLipid 4: no polar head, three C16 acyl chains\nWhich pairing of lipid and structure formed is correct?',choices:['Lipid 4 forms a micelle','Lipid 2 forms a bilayer','Lipid 3 forms an oil droplet','Lipid 1 forms a bilayer'],ans:3,exp:'Shape decides the assembly. **One polar head + two tails** is roughly cylindrical and packs into a **bilayer** (Lipid 1). **One head + one tail** is cone-shaped and packs into a **micelle**. **No head** gives an **oil droplet**.\n\nLipid 2 forms a bilayer: a single-tailed lysophospholipid is cone-shaped and forms micelles.\nLipid 3 forms an oil droplet: its charged sulfate head makes it a detergent that forms micelles.\nLipid 4 forms a micelle: with no polar head it cannot face water, so it forms oil droplets.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C8',from:'pe6-cp:44'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Sodium dodecyl sulfate is added above its critical micelle concentration to water that contains a small amount of a nonpolar dye, which is insoluble in pure water. The dye becomes evenly dispersed. Where is the dye located?',choices:['Between the two leaflets of a bilayer','Bound to the sulfate groups at the surface','Dissolved in the water between micelles','In the hydrocarbon cores of the micelles'],ans:3,exp:'SDS has **one nonpolar C12 tail and a charged head**, so above its critical micelle concentration it forms **micelles**: heads outward to water, tails packed into a **hydrocarbon core**. A nonpolar dye dissolves in that core (like dissolves like), which disperses it.\n\nBound to the sulfate groups: the charged surface is polar, a poor home for a nonpolar dye.\nIn the water between micelles: the dye is insoluble in water, as stated.\nBetween two leaflets of a bilayer: single-tailed SDS forms micelles, not bilayers.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C9',from:'pe5-cp:23'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Three detergents share the same sugar head group and differ only in the length of their saturated tail. Their critical micelle concentrations (CMC) are:\nC8 tail: 25 mM\nC10 tail: 2.2 mM\nC12 tail: 0.17 mM\nWhich explanation best accounts for the trend?',choices:['Longer tails are less water-soluble','Longer tails make the head more polar','Longer tails hydrogen bond more with water','Shorter tails pack into micelles more easily'],ans:0,exp:'Micelles form when monomers become **less stable in water than in a micelle core**. Each added CH₂ makes the tail **more nonpolar and less water-soluble**, so the molecules aggregate at a **lower concentration**: the CMC falls about tenfold per two carbons.\n\nMore polar head: the head group is the same in all three.\nHydrogen bond more with water: hydrocarbon tails do not hydrogen bond; more tail means less affinity for water.\nShorter tails pack more easily: then the C8 detergent would have the lowest CMC, but it has the highest.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C10',from:'pe4-cp:47'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Four lipid-derived signaling molecules are described:\nCompound W: C₁₈H₂₄O₂; four fused rings (three six-membered, one five-membered)\nCompound X: C₂₀H₃₄O₅; one five-membered ring with two long side chains\nCompound Y: C₁₈H₃₇NO₂; an unbranched amino diol chain, no rings\nCompound Z: C₂₀H₃₀O; one six-membered ring and a polyene chain\nWhich compound is a steroid?',choices:['W','X','Y','Z'],ans:0,exp:'Steroids are made from cholesterol and keep its core: **four fused rings, three six-membered and one five-membered**. Only W has it (its formula matches estradiol).\n\nCompound X: a single five-membered ring with two chains is a prostaglandin, made from a C20 fatty acid.\nCompound Y: an open amino diol chain is sphingosine, the backbone of sphingolipids.\nCompound Z: one ring plus a polyene chain describes retinol (vitamin A), an isoprenoid but not a steroid.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C11',from:'pe3-cp:25'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Cholesterol is added to vesicles made of phosphatidylcholine and inserts into the bilayer. How is it oriented?',choices:['–OH at the head groups, rings among the tails','Rings at the surface, –OH at the midplane','Lying flat on the outer surface of the bilayer','Outside the bilayer, held by its –OH'],ans:0,exp:'Cholesterol is **amphipathic but mostly nonpolar**: one small polar –OH and a rigid four-ring hydrocarbon body with a short tail. It sits **parallel to the phospholipids**, with the **–OH near the polar head groups** (where it can hydrogen bond to water and ester carbonyls) and the **rings among the acyl chains**.\n\nRings at the surface: this puts the polar –OH in the nonpolar core and the hydrocarbon in water.\nLying flat on the surface: this would expose the large hydrocarbon body to water.\nOutside the bilayer: one –OH cannot keep 27 carbons in water.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C12',from:'pe6-cp:44'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5B',q:'A fatty acid–binding protein holds palmitate (C₁₅H₃₁COO⁻) with the carboxylate near the pocket entrance and the hydrocarbon tail deep inside. Which residues most likely line the entrance and the deep pocket?',choices:['Arginine at the entrance; isoleucine deep','Isoleucine at the entrance; arginine deep','Aspartate at the entrance; serine deep','Glutamate at the entrance; lysine deep'],ans:0,exp:'Match each part of the ligand. The **negative carboxylate** is best held by a **positive side chain (arginine)** through a salt bridge. The **15-carbon tail** is best surrounded by **nonpolar side chains (isoleucine)** through London dispersion forces.\n\nIsoleucine at the entrance, arginine deep: reversed; a charged arginine against the tail is unfavorable.\nAspartate at the entrance: its negative charge repels the carboxylate; serine is polar.\nGlutamate at the entrance: also negative; lysine\'s charge does not suit the hydrocarbon tail.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C13',from:'pe1-cp:31'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Each of four deoxynucleoside monophosphates is synthesized with every nitrogen atom as ¹⁵N instead of ¹⁴N. One labeled product is heavier than its unlabeled form by about 2 Da. Which compound is it?',choices:['dTMP','dCMP','dAMP','dGMP'],ans:0,exp:'Only the base contains N (deoxyribose and phosphate have none), and each ¹⁵N adds about 1 Da. So the base has **2 N**.\n**Purines** (A, G) have two fused rings with 4 ring N, plus an exocyclic N on A (NH₂) and G (NH₂): **5 N each**.\n**Pyrimidines** have one ring with 2 ring N: cytosine adds an NH₂ (**3 N**); **thymine has no amino group (2 N)**.\n\ndCMP: +3 Da, since cytosine\'s amino group adds a third N.\ndAMP: +5 Da, a purine.\ndGMP: +5 Da, also a purine.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C14',from:'pe6-cp:45'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'The base compositions (mol %) of four DNA samples are:\nSample 1: A 30, T 30, G 20, C 20\nSample 2: A 18, T 18, G 32, C 32\nSample 3: A 25, T 33, G 24, C 18\nSample 4: A 35, T 35, G 15, C 15\nWhich sample is most likely single-stranded?',choices:['3','1','2','4'],ans:0,exp:'In double-stranded DNA every A pairs with a T and every G with a C (**Chargaff: A = T, G = C**), so purines = pyrimidines. Sample 3 breaks both equalities (A 25 vs T 33; G 24 vs C 18), so its bases are not all paired.\n\nSample 1: A = T and G = C, consistent with a duplex.\nSample 2: GC-rich, but still A = T and G = C.\nSample 4: AT-rich, but A = T and G = C; a high A content alone does not mean single-stranded.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C15',from:'pe4-cp:51'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Interbase hydrogen bonds are counted in a fully paired 30-base-pair DNA duplex, and the total is 72. Assuming Watson–Crick pairing, how many of its base pairs are G–C pairs?',choices:['12','24','18','6'],ans:0,exp:'Every pair has at least 2 H-bonds (A–T has **2**), and each G–C pair adds **one more (3)**. Thirty pairs at 2 each give 60, so the 72 − 60 = **12 extra H-bonds mean 12 G–C pairs** (check: 12 × 3 + 18 × 2 = 36 + 36 = 72).\n\n24: divides 72 by 3, as if every bond belonged to a G–C pair.\n18: the number of A–T pairs, not G–C pairs.\n6: divides the 12 extra bonds by 2, as if each G–C pair added two.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C16',from:'pe4-cp:51'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'Three 40-base-pair DNA duplexes have the same buffer and differ only in G–C content. Their melting temperatures are:\n25% G–C: 62 °C\n50% G–C: 71 °C\n75% G–C: 80 °C\nWhich conclusion is best supported?',choices:['G–C pairs hold the strands together more strongly','A–T pairs hold the strands together more strongly','G–C pairs are joined by covalent bonds','Melting temperature depends only on length'],ans:0,exp:'Tm rises steadily with G–C content at fixed length, so **more energy is needed to separate G–C-rich strands**. A G–C pair has **3 hydrogen bonds versus 2 for A–T**, and G–C stacking is also stronger.\n\nA–T pairs stronger: then Tm would fall as G–C content rises, the opposite of the data.\nCovalent G–C bonds: base pairs are noncovalent; that is why the strands separate at 60–80 °C.\nDepends only on length: all three are 40 bp, yet their Tm values differ by 18 °C.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C17',from:'pe4-cp:51'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'One strand of a DNA fragment is 30% A, 20% T, 35% G and 15% C. Its partner strand is synthesized to give a fully paired duplex. What percentage of all bases in the duplex is guanine?',choices:['25%','35%','15%','50%'],ans:0,exp:'Pairing makes the new strand **C 35% and G 15%** (each template G puts a C opposite it, each template C a G). The two strands are the same length, so the duplex G is the average: (35 + 15) ÷ 2 = **25%**. Check with Chargaff: G = C, and G + C = 50% of the duplex.\n\n35%: the G of the first strand alone, ignoring the second strand.\n15%: the G of the new strand alone.\n50%: G + C together, not G alone.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C18',from:'pe3-cp:44'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A region of a DNA template strand has the composition A 40%, T 10%, G 30%, C 20%. Which composition does the RNA transcribed from it have?',choices:['U 40%, A 10%, C 30%, G 20%','T 40%, A 10%, C 30%, G 20%','A 40%, U 10%, G 30%, C 20%','U 40%, A 10%, G 30%, C 20%'],ans:0,exp:'The transcript is **complementary** to the template, and RNA uses **uracil in place of thymine**: template A → U, T → A, G → C, C → G. So U 40%, A 10%, C 30%, G 20%.\n\nT 40%: right pairing, but RNA contains U, not T.\nA 40%, U 10%: copies the template (swapping T for U) instead of pairing with it.\nU 40%, A 10%, G 30%, C 20%: pairs A and T correctly but copies G and C unpaired.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C19',from:'pe3-cp:44'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A nucleotide analog is identical to dATP except that C3′ of the sugar carries an H instead of an –OH. After DNA polymerase adds it to a growing strand, what happens?',choices:['Synthesis stops; no 3′-OH can attack the next dNTP','Synthesis continues; the 5′-phosphate forms the next bond','Synthesis stops; the analog cannot pair with thymine','Synthesis continues; the 2′-OH forms the next bond'],ans:0,exp:'DNA grows **5′ → 3′**: the **3′-OH** at the end of the strand attacks the α-phosphate of the incoming dNTP, forming a **3′→5′ phosphodiester** and releasing PPi. With H at C3′ there is no nucleophile, so the chain terminates (the basis of dideoxy sequencing).\n\n5′-phosphate forms the next bond: the 5′ end of the analog was used to attach it; the next bond needs its 3′-OH.\nCannot pair with thymine: the base is unchanged adenine, which pairs normally; that is how it was added.\n2′-OH forms the next bond: a deoxynucleotide has no 2′-OH.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C20',from:'pe1-cp:40'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'At pH 13 and 37 °C, RNA is cleaved into mononucleotides within hours, while DNA under the same conditions stays intact. Which structural difference best explains this?',choices:['RNA\'s sugar has a 2′-OH next to each phosphate','RNA contains uracil in place of thymine','RNA is usually single-stranded rather than paired','RNA\'s phosphodiesters link C2′ rather than C3′'],ans:0,exp:'In base, the **2′-OH of ribose** is deprotonated and attacks the **neighboring phosphorus** of its own 3′,5′-phosphodiester, cutting the backbone. Deoxyribose has **no 2′-OH**, so DNA lacks this internal nucleophile.\n\nUracil in place of thymine: the bases are not part of the backbone that is cleaved.\nSingle-stranded: single-stranded DNA is also stable in base; pairing is not the issue.\nLinks at C2′: both RNA and DNA backbones join C3′ of one sugar to C5′ of the next.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C21',from:'pe1-cp:40'},
  {s:'Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5D',q:'A DNA strand has the sequence 5′-GATTCC-3′. What is the sequence of its complementary strand, written 5′ to 3′?',choices:['5′-GGAATC-3′','5′-CTAAGG-3′','5′-CCTTAG-3′','5′-GGAAUC-3′'],ans:0,exp:'The strands are **antiparallel**. Pair each base (G↔C, A↔T): under 5′-GATTCC-3′ the partner reads **3′-CTAAGG-5′**. Rewritten 5′ → 3′, it is **5′-GGAATC-3′**.\n\n5′-CTAAGG-3′: correct bases, but the 3′ and 5′ ends are mislabeled (the strands would be parallel).\n5′-CCTTAG-3′: the original sequence reversed but not complemented.\n5′-GGAAUC-3′: uses uracil, which is found in RNA, not DNA.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C22',from:'pe3-cp:44'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'An isomerase reaction, A ⇌ B, is started with pure A at 25 °C and allowed to reach equilibrium. The final concentrations are [A] = 2.0 mM and [B] = 0.020 mM. What is ΔG°′ for A → B? (At 25 °C, each factor of 10 in K corresponds to about 5.7 kJ/mol.)',choices:['+11 kJ/mol','−11 kJ/mol','+5.7 kJ/mol','+23 kJ/mol'],ans:0,exp:'**K = [B]/[A]** = 0.020 ÷ 2.0 = **10⁻²**. **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K = −5.7 × log K** = −5.7 × (−2) ≈ **+11 kJ/mol**. Positive, as expected when reactant dominates at equilibrium.\n\n−11 kJ/mol: uses [A]/[B] (K inverted), which flips the sign.\n+5.7 kJ/mol: counts one factor of 10 instead of two.\n+23 kJ/mol: counts four factors of 10, as if K were 10⁻⁴.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C23',from:'pe3-cp:41'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'At 37 °C, reaction 1 has ΔG°′ = −22.8 kJ/mol and reaction 2 has ΔG°′ = −11.4 kJ/mol. What is the ratio K₁/K₂ of their equilibrium constants? (About 5.7 kJ/mol corresponds to a factor of 10 in K.)',choices:['100','2','10','0.01'],ans:0,exp:'**ΔG°′ = −RT ln K**, so each −5.7 kJ/mol multiplies K by 10. The two reactions differ by 22.8 − 11.4 = **11.4 kJ/mol = 2 × 5.7**, so K₁ is **10² = 100** times K₂ (K₁ ≈ 10⁴, K₂ ≈ 10²).\n\n2: divides the ΔG°′ values; K depends exponentially on ΔG°′, not proportionally.\n10: counts only one factor of 10.\n0.01: inverts the ratio; the more negative ΔG°′ has the larger K.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C24',from:'pe3-cp:55'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A drug binds its receptor with a dissociation constant Kd = 1 μM at 25 °C. Using a 1 M standard state, approximately what is ΔG° for binding (drug + receptor → complex)? (Each factor of 10 in K ≈ 5.7 kJ/mol.)',choices:['−34 kJ/mol','+34 kJ/mol','−17 kJ/mol','−5.7 kJ/mol'],ans:0,exp:'Binding is the reverse of dissociation, so **K(binding) = 1/Kd** = 1/(10⁻⁶ M) = **10⁶**. ΔG° = −5.7 × log K = −5.7 × 6 ≈ **−34 kJ/mol**: tight binding is strongly favorable.\n\n+34 kJ/mol: uses Kd itself (10⁻⁶) as the binding constant, which is the dissociation direction.\n−17 kJ/mol: treats 1 μM as 10⁻³ M (a μ/m slip).\n−5.7 kJ/mol: counts a single factor of 10.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C25',from:'pe3-cp:55'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A reaction S → P has ΔG°′ = +5.7 kJ/mol at 25 °C (K = 0.10). In a cell, [P]/[S] is held at 0.0010. What is ΔG for the reaction in the cell, and which way does it run? (RT ln 10 ≈ 5.7 kJ/mol)',choices:['−11 kJ/mol; it runs forward','+5.7 kJ/mol; it does not run forward','+23 kJ/mol; it runs in reverse','0 kJ/mol; it is at equilibrium'],ans:0,exp:'**ΔG = ΔG°′ + RT ln Q**, with Q = [P]/[S] = 10⁻³. RT ln Q = 5.7 × log(10⁻³) = **−17 kJ/mol**. ΔG = +5.7 − 17 ≈ **−11 kJ/mol**, so the reaction runs forward. Q (10⁻³) is below K (10⁻¹), which says the same thing.\n\n+5.7 kJ/mol: that is ΔG°′, which applies only when all concentrations are 1 M; the cell is not at standard state.\n+23 kJ/mol: adds 17 instead of subtracting (sign slip on log Q).\n0 kJ/mol: equilibrium would need Q = K = 0.10, not 0.0010.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C26',from:'pe3-cp:41'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A step in glycolysis has ΔG°′ ≈ +24 kJ/mol, yet in cells it proceeds steadily in the forward direction. Which explanation is most consistent?',choices:['Low product levels make its ΔG negative','Its enzyme lowers ΔG°′ to a negative value','Body temperature makes its ΔG°′ negative','A fast rate overrides its positive ΔG'],ans:0,exp:'Direction is set by **ΔG = ΔG°′ + RT ln Q**, not by ΔG°′ alone. The next enzymes remove the products quickly, keeping **Q far below K**, so RT ln Q is large and negative and **ΔG < 0**.\n\nEnzyme lowers ΔG°′: catalysts lower the activation energy only; ΔG°′ and K are unchanged.\nBody temperature: ΔG°′ is defined at a stated temperature, and 37 °C is close to 25 °C; it cannot turn +24 negative.\nFast rate overrides ΔG: rate never sets direction; a reaction with ΔG > 0 runs in reverse no matter how fast.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C27',from:'pe3-cp:41'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'The conversion S → P was measured at 37 °C with and without an enzyme:\nNo enzyme: initial rate 0.002 μM/s; equilibrium [P]/[S] = 50\nWith enzyme: initial rate 40 μM/s; equilibrium [P]/[S] = 50\nWhich conclusion is supported by these data?',choices:['The enzyme raises the rate but not ΔG°′','The enzyme lowers ΔG°′, which raises the rate','The enzyme raises K, which raises the rate','The enzyme lowers ΔG°′ but leaves Ea unchanged'],ans:0,exp:'The **rate rose 20,000-fold** (0.002 → 40 μM/s), but the **equilibrium ratio, K, is 50 in both**. Since ΔG°′ = −RT ln K, **ΔG°′ is the same**. An enzyme lowers the activation energy, speeding both directions equally, without changing where equilibrium lies.\n\nLowers ΔG°′: K is unchanged, so ΔG°′ is unchanged.\nRaises K: K is 50 with and without the enzyme.\nLowers ΔG°′ but not Ea: reversed; the rate change comes from a lower Ea, and ΔG°′ stays fixed.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C28',from:'pe1-cp:12'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A reaction with ΔG°′ = −20 kJ/mol is run in a sealed tube at constant temperature until it reaches equilibrium. Which statement is true at equilibrium?',choices:['ΔG = 0 and ΔG°′ = −20 kJ/mol','ΔG°′ = 0 and ΔG is −20 kJ/mol','ΔG = 0 and ΔG°′ = 0','ΔG = −20 kJ/mol and K = 1'],ans:0,exp:'**ΔG°′** is a fixed property of the reaction (at that temperature), tied to K by ΔG°′ = −RT ln K. **ΔG** depends on the current concentrations: ΔG = ΔG°′ + RT ln Q. At equilibrium Q = K, so **ΔG = 0**, while ΔG°′ stays −20 kJ/mol.\n\nΔG°′ = 0, ΔG = −20: swaps the two quantities.\nBoth zero: ΔG°′ does not change as concentrations change.\nΔG = −20 and K = 1: a negative ΔG would mean the reaction is still moving forward; and ΔG°′ = −20 means K ≈ 3000, not 1.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C29',from:'pe3-cp:41'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Hexokinase couples two reactions:\nglucose + Pi → glucose-6-phosphate + H₂O, ΔG°′ = +14 kJ/mol\nATP + H₂O → ADP + Pi, ΔG°′ = −30 kJ/mol\nWhat is ΔG°′ for the overall reaction, glucose + ATP → glucose-6-phosphate + ADP?',choices:['−16 kJ/mol','+44 kJ/mol','−44 kJ/mol','+16 kJ/mol'],ans:0,exp:'Adding the two equations cancels Pi and H₂O and gives the overall reaction. **ΔG is a state function, so coupled ΔG°′ values add**: +14 + (−30) = **−16 kJ/mol**. The favorable ATP hydrolysis pays for the unfavorable phosphorylation.\n\n+44 kJ/mol: subtracts the ATP value (14 − (−30)) instead of adding it.\n−44 kJ/mol: treats both steps as favorable (−14 − 30).\n+16 kJ/mol: correct magnitude, wrong sign.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C30',from:'pe5-cp:8'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'A biosynthetic step has ΔG°′ = +45 kJ/mol. It can be coupled to the hydrolysis of ATP to ADP + Pi (ΔG°′ ≈ −30 kJ/mol per ATP). What is the smallest number of ATP that must be hydrolyzed per reaction to make the overall ΔG°′ negative?',choices:['Two','One','Three','Zero'],ans:0,exp:'Coupled ΔG°′ values add. With **one ATP**: +45 − 30 = **+15 kJ/mol**, still unfavorable. With **two ATP**: +45 − 60 = **−15 kJ/mol**, favorable. So two is the minimum.\n\nOne: leaves the overall ΔG°′ at +15 kJ/mol.\nThree: also gives a negative sum (−45 kJ/mol), but it is not the smallest number that works.\nZero: an enzyme cannot change ΔG°′; it only speeds the reaction.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C31',from:'pe5-cp:8'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Two reactions run in sequence at 37 °C:\nA → B, K₁ = 10⁻²\nB → C, K₂ = 10⁵\nWhat are the equilibrium constant and ΔG°′ for the overall reaction A → C? (Each factor of 10 in K ≈ 5.7 kJ/mol.)',choices:['K = 10³; ΔG°′ ≈ −17 kJ/mol','K = 10³; ΔG°′ ≈ +17 kJ/mol','K = 10⁷; ΔG°′ ≈ −40 kJ/mol','K = 10⁵; ΔG°′ ≈ −29 kJ/mol'],ans:0,exp:'When reactions are added, their **ΔG°′ values add and their K values multiply**: K = 10⁻² × 10⁵ = **10³**. ΔG°′ = −5.7 × 3 ≈ **−17 kJ/mol** (check: +11.4 + (−28.5) ≈ −17).\n\nK = 10³, +17 kJ/mol: right K, but K > 1 must give a negative ΔG°′.\nK = 10⁷, −40 kJ/mol: adds the exponents\' magnitudes (2 + 5), ignoring the sign of 10⁻².\nK = 10⁵, −29 kJ/mol: uses only the second step and ignores the unfavorable first one.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C32',from:'pe3-cp:55'},
  {s:'Bioenergetics',disc:'biochemistry',aamc:'5E',q:'Under typical cell conditions, one cycle of the Na⁺/K⁺ pump (moving 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in against their gradients) requires about +40 kJ/mol. Hydrolysis of ATP has ΔG°′ ≈ −30 kJ/mol, but at cellular concentrations of ATP, ADP and Pi its ΔG is about −50 kJ/mol. Is one ATP per cycle enough, and why?',choices:['Yes; the summed ΔG is about −10 kJ/mol','No; the summed ΔG is about +10 kJ/mol','Yes; coupled ΔG values multiply, not add','No; transport is paid for by ΔG°′, not ΔG'],ans:0,exp:'In a cell what matters is the **actual ΔG** of each coupled process, which **add**: +40 + (−50) ≈ **−10 kJ/mol**, so one ATP per cycle is enough. ATP\'s cellular ΔG is more negative than ΔG°′ because [ATP] is kept far above equilibrium relative to [ADP][Pi].\n\nNo, +10 kJ/mol: uses the standard −30 kJ/mol instead of the cellular −50.\nΔG values multiply: equilibrium constants multiply; free energies add.\nPaid for by ΔG°′: ΔG°′ describes 1 M standard conditions; the cell runs on actual ΔG.',src:'cp-gap-bch:C33',from:'pe5-cp:8'},
];
